\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


For an integer $n\geq2$, put $S(n)=\sum_{a=1}^{n-1}a^{n-1}$. Divisibility $n\mid S(n)+1$ means $S(n)\equiv-1\pmod n$. A prime integer is an integer greater than one with no positive divisors other than one and itself.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Is the equivalence
\[\forall n\geq2,\qquad n\mid\left(1+\sum_{a=1}^{n-1}a^{n-1}\right)\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad n\text{ is prime}\]
true? In particular, does the displayed divisibility condition rule out every composite integer? \href{https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0409259}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Does the power-sum congruence characterize primes, with no composite integer satisfying it?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, \emph{UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems}, record 1870 (GUY-A17). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Bernd C. Kellner, \emph{The Equivalence of Giuga’s and Agoh’s Conjectures}, arXiv:math/0409259 (2004). Section1 and Conjectures2.1--2.2, pages1--2. \url{https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0409259}.
